# packages/images/agent/container-hardening.env — the canonical
# container-hardening contract for the blitzlog-agent container.
#
# Issue #164 ("harden the agent container — non-root, dropped
# capabilities, read-only rootfs").
#
# This file is the SINGLE SOURCE OF TRUTH for the hardening flags that
# every `docker run` of the agent image must carry. It is consumed by:
#
#   * the host-side watchdog
#     (infra/aws/packer/scripts-docker-ubuntu/watchdog.sh), which is
#     baked into the EC2 AMI as /usr/local/bin/container-hardening.env
#     by the Packer `file` provisioner + the `cp` in 02-systemd.sh;
#   * the local dev tasks in mise.toml (`mise run image-*`), which
#     source it straight out of the repo.
#
# It is a POSIX `KEY=value` file with NO code — no arrays, no bashisms —
# so it can be sourced by `sh` (dash) as well as `bash`. Every value
# uses `:=` so an operator can override it from the environment.
#
# tests/test_container_hardening.py asserts that:
#   * the flags below are carried by EVERY `docker run` site (the
#     watchdog + all five mise dev tasks);
#   * the writable-path list matches the table in docs/DOCKER.md;
#   * the uid/gid/home below agree with the Dockerfile.
#
# The list cannot silently grow: adding a writable path here without
# documenting it in docs/DOCKER.md fails the test suite.

# --- Identity of the container user -------------------------------------
# The container runs as uid 0 (the image has no `USER` directive), and
# that is load-bearing rather than an oversight:
#
#   * dpkg needs root — it unpacks files into /usr, writes its own
#     database in /var/lib/dpkg and runs maintainer scripts. A project's
#     `bootstrap` task installs its own build dependencies with apt
#     (the documented pattern in docs/BOOTSTRAP.md), so the container
#     has to be able to install packages. See issue #174.
#   * the container has NO capabilities (`--cap-drop=ALL`), so uid 0 is
#     a capability-less root: it cannot bypass file permissions
#     (CAP_DAC_OVERRIDE is gone), cannot mount anything
#     (CAP_SYS_ADMIN is gone) and cannot escalate (CAP_SETUID is gone).
#
# The host-side watchdog chowns the bind mounts it exposes to the
# container to this uid/gid. With uid 0 that is a no-op on the host —
# but it is kept, because a bind mount preserves the SOURCE's
# ownership, and the uid in the contract is the single source of truth
# for "who owns the paths the container writes to".
#
# The uid also has to match the owner of the tmpfs mounts below: with
# `--cap-drop=ALL` there is no CAP_DAC_OVERRIDE, so a uid-0 process
# cannot write into a directory owned by another uid (the kernel applies
# the "other" permission bits to it) — a 0700 uid-1000 tmpfs would be
# unwritable for the agent.
: "${BLITZLOG_CONTAINER_UID:=0}"
: "${BLITZLOG_CONTAINER_GID:=0}"

# The agent's HOME inside the container. It is a tmpfs (see the mounts
# below), so the agent's state — the global git config, mise's
# toolchain, the npm cache, opencode's config and state — never lands
# in the container's writable layer.
: "${BLITZLOG_CONTAINER_HOME:=/home/blitzlog}"

# --- Tier 1: inert hardening flags -------------------------------------
# Applied to every `docker run` of the agent image.
#
#   --security-opt=no-new-privileges:true  blocks setuid elevation, so
#                                           third-party npm code that
#                                           runs in-container cannot
#                                           gain privileges.
#   --cap-drop=ALL                         drops Docker's default
#                                           capability set (CAP_NET_RAW,
#                                           CAP_SYS_CHROOT,
#                                           CAP_DAC_OVERRIDE,
#                                           CAP_MKNOD, CAP_SETUID,
#                                           CAP_SETGID). The base agent
#                                           path needs none of them.
#   --pids-limit                           bounds the process count so
#                                           a fork bomb cannot exhaust
#                                           the host's PIDs.
#   --init                                 runs docker-init as PID 1 so
#                                           orphaned grandchildren are
#                                           reaped instead of piling up
#                                           as zombies for the life of
#                                           the run.
#
# What is NOT here is `--read-only` (a read-only root filesystem).
# The rootfs has to stay writable: a project's `bootstrap` task
# installs its own build dependencies, and dpkg cannot unpack a package
# into a read-only /usr or write its database into a read-only
# /var/lib/dpkg. See issue #174 for the trade-off — the capability drop
# above is what bounds the agent inside the container, and the
# container's blast radius is small anyway (no AWS credentials, the
# credential mounts stay read-only, and only the agent's own workspace
# and log directory are shared with the host).
: "${BLITZLOG_PIDS_LIMIT:=1024}"
BLITZLOG_HARDENING_FLAGS="--security-opt=no-new-privileges:true --cap-drop=ALL --pids-limit=${BLITZLOG_PIDS_LIMIT} --init"

# --- The writable-path contract -----------------------------------------
# The rootfs itself is writable (see the note on --read-only above), so
# this list is no longer "every path the agent may write to". It is the
# set of paths that are kept OUT of the container's writable layer, and
# it is:
#
#   1. explicit — it is the complete set, not a sample;
#   2. documented — the table in docs/DOCKER.md mirrors it verbatim;
#   3. asserted — tests/test_container_hardening.py fails if the two
#      drift apart, so the list cannot silently grow.
#
# `/home/blitzlog` is a tmpfs because it is the agent's HOME: the global
# git config ($HOME/.gitconfig), mise's toolchain ($HOME/.local), the
# npm/npx cache ($HOME/.npm), opencode's own state ($HOME/.local/share,
# $HOME/.cache) and the opencode + telegram-bot config dirs
# ($HOME/.config) all live under it. A tmpfs means none of that lands
# in the container's writable layer — the agent's state dies with the
# container. ($HOME/.config additionally carries a nested tmpfs — see
# BLITZLOG_TMPFS_MOUNTS below for why.)
#
# `/tmp` is a tmpfs for scratch space (pip/npm/node temporaries).
#
# `/var/log/blitzlog` and `/workspace` are bind mounts from the host —
# they are writable because the agent must write logs (the host uploads
# them to S3) and must clone/edit/commit/push the repository. The host
# chowns both to the container's uid.
#
# `${BLITZLOG_CONTAINER_HOME}/.config/gh` is a bind mount too, and is
# writable on purpose (issue #176). The host owns it and rewrites
# `hosts.yml` in place on every token rotation (issue #145), so the
# agent always sees a current token. It is NOT read-only because gh
# 2.x migrates its own config on first use — with no `config.yml` next
# to it, gh wants to WRITE `hosts.yml`, and against a read-only mount
# every `gh` call in the container fails. The agent worked around that
# by copying the config to a writable directory, which silently breaks
# the token refresh the copy was meant to demonstrate.
#
# Making it writable costs nothing: the AMI's host extras are
# awscli/git/jq (the host runs no `gh` of its own), so that directory
# exists only for the container, and the agent already holds the token.
# The GIT credential store stays read-only — `git credential-store`
# genuinely rewrites that file, which is why the container ships a
# get-only credential helper instead.
BLITZLOG_WRITABLE_PATHS="/tmp ${BLITZLOG_CONTAINER_HOME} /var/log/blitzlog /workspace ${BLITZLOG_CONTAINER_HOME}/.config/gh"

# The tmpfs mounts that back the writable paths above. Stored as
# ready-to-splat flag strings.
#
# `$HOME/.config` gets its OWN tmpfs, nested inside the `$HOME` one.
# It looks redundant — it is inside a writable path already — but it
# keeps opencode's config (and the telegram bot's) out of the
# container's writable layer, and it gives the mount point a known
# owner:
#
# Docker creates the MISSING PARENT of a bind mount as `root:root 0755`.
# The gh CLI config is bind-mounted at `$HOME/.config/gh`, so Docker
# creates `$HOME/.config` itself — and it lands INSIDE the `$HOME`
# tmpfs. Mounting a tmpfs at `$HOME/.config` with the container's own
# uid/gid makes the mountpoint uid-owned, and Docker then mounts the
# `$HOME/.config/gh` bind INSIDE it (parents are mounted before
# children). The bind's own mountpoint stays root-owned, which is
# correct: that one is read-only.
#
# The rule this encodes: ANY mount point under `$HOME` needs its own
# tmpfs, because Docker will otherwise create its parent as root —
# and with `--cap-drop=ALL` the container's uid 0 cannot fall back on
# CAP_DAC_OVERRIDE to write into a directory owned by somebody else.
#
# `exec` is REQUIRED on all three mounts, and it is not the default:
#
# Docker's `--tmpfs` mounts a tmpfs with `noexec,nosuid,nodev` unless
# the option string says otherwise (moby prepends those defaults and
# merges the user's options on top — moby/sys `MergeTmpfsOptions`).
# `nosuid` and `nodev` we want; `noexec` we must NOT have, because
# these mounts are where the agent's executables live:
#
#   * mise's toolchain — `mise install` puts node, python, terraform
#     and friends under `$HOME/.local/share/mise`, and the agent has
#     to RUN them;
#   * the opencode tools (the `shutdown` tool) under
#     `$HOME/.config/opencode/tools`;
#   * `/tmp` — pip, npm and node-gyp compile and RUN scripts there.
#
# With Docker's default `noexec` in place, the very first `mise` call
# died with
#
#   /usr/local/bin/entrypoint.sh: 653: mise: Permission denied
#
# (dash exits 126) — the container died before the agent started, and
# the toolchain it installs would have been unusable too. `exec` clears
# MS_NOEXEC; `nosuid` and `nodev` stay.
#
# The image's smoke test proves the mounts are exec-able: it installs a
# binary under `/tmp` and runs it.
BLITZLOG_TMPFS_MOUNTS="--tmpfs /tmp:rw,nosuid,nodev,exec,mode=1777 --tmpfs ${BLITZLOG_CONTAINER_HOME}:rw,nosuid,nodev,exec,uid=${BLITZLOG_CONTAINER_UID},gid=${BLITZLOG_CONTAINER_GID},mode=0700 --tmpfs ${BLITZLOG_CONTAINER_HOME}/.config:rw,nosuid,nodev,exec,uid=${BLITZLOG_CONTAINER_UID},gid=${BLITZLOG_CONTAINER_GID},mode=0700"

# Bind-mounted paths the container may only READ. The GitHub credential
# store is owned by the HOST and rewritten there by the token-refresh
# poll loop (issue #145), so the container sees it read-only and cannot
# tamper with the host's copy — `git credential-store` would rewrite
# it on every authenticated operation, which is why the container
# ships a get-only credential helper instead.
#
# The gh CLI config is NOT in this list (issue #176): gh migrates its
# own config on first use and would fail against a read-only mount.
# It is in BLITZLOG_WRITABLE_PATHS instead — see the comment there.
BLITZLOG_READONLY_MOUNTS="/home/blitzlog/.git-credentials.d /opt/whisper-stt/models"

# --- Tier 3: the tailnet capability exemption -------------------------
# The ONLY relaxation of --cap-drop=ALL in the whole project.
#
# tailscaled needs CAP_NET_ADMIN to create and configure the WireGuard
# interface and CAP_NET_RAW for `tailscale ping`'s ICMP probes, plus
# the /dev/net/tun character device. It also needs a writable path for
# its state and its control socket.
#
# These flags are applied ONLY when TAILSCALE_AUTH_KEY is set — i.e.
# only on runs that actually enrol in a Tailnet. Every other run gets
# --cap-drop=ALL and nothing else.
#
# The container-side Tailnet join is the ONLY enrolment path: the
# Lambda bootstrap no longer installs or joins Tailscale on the host
# (the old `_install_tailscale_script` is gone), so dropping the
# in-container join would remove the local-LLM-over-Tailnet transport
# outright. See issue #164 for the full decision record.
BLITZLOG_TAILSCALE_FLAGS="--cap-add=NET_ADMIN --cap-add=NET_RAW --device /dev/net/tun"
